    "combat_read": "## COMBAT BRAIN\nFires when combat is underway -- physical or magical attacks being exchanged or initiated. Combat intents use the structured schema fields (type, targets, damageType, impact, buff), not the social pipe grammar. Vocalization -- a shout while charging, a warning while shielding -- is folded into the action text, not a separate intent.\n\nINTENT COUNTS BY TIER\nTier sets how many intents a combatant gets and their raw power:\n- trivial/weak: 1 simple intent\n- average: 1-2 intents\n- strong: 2 intents, should use abilities\n- elite/boss/mythic: 3-4 intents, tactical and dramatic\nCompanions use the same tier-based counts as enemies. Every combatant acts each round; the non-combat who-gets-intents gate does not apply here.\n\nSTEP 1: READ THE NPC\nBefore writing any intent, check:\n- Abilities: what can they actually do? Abilities are more interesting and more effective than a generic swing -- default to them.\n- hiddenInfo combat style: how do they fight -- range, aggression, signature moves, what they fear?\n- Personality: a coward, a zealot, a protector, and an opportunist each fight differently. Character drives combat.\n- Current state: HP, buffs and debuffs, position, and what happened to them last round. React to it -- never generate as if the previous round did not happen.\n\nSTEP 2: READ THE BATTLEFIELD -- NOT JUST THE PLAYER\nCombat is not \"everyone swings at the player.\" Read the whole field:\n- Biggest threat: highest damage, an active caster, a healer sustaining the other side -- often NOT the player.\n- Most vulnerable: low HP, isolated, out of position, mid-cast.\n- What just changed: someone dropped, a formation broke, a buff went up, terrain shifted.\n- What THIS combatant cares about: a companion shields its bonded ally; a bandit guards its escape route; a beast defends territory or its young; a fanatic goes for the priest. Character sets priorities, and those priorities frequently point away from the player.\nTarget selection follows from this read. An enemy focuses the companion healer because she is the real threat; two thugs gang the isolated party member; a mother beast ignores everyone to defend its nest. Do NOT tunnel the player by default -- a field where every enemy fixates on the player and ignores the companions is the most common failure. Spread attention as real combatants would.",
    "combat_conduct": "## SAPIENCE -- HOW SMART IS THIS COMBATANT\nTier is power. SAPIENCE is intelligence, and it is a SEPARATE axis: it sets tactical sophistication and the behavior repertoire. A powerful beast can be dumb; a weak soldier can be sharp. Read both, every fight.\n\n- MINDLESS / INSTINCT (most animals, oozes, simple constructs): go for the nearest or the weakest, fight until hurt, then flee or panic. Guard territory or young. No planning, no real teamwork beyond a swarm, no abilities used as tactics. Fear and pain drive them, not strategy.\n- ANIMAL CUNNING (pack hunters, clever beasts, feral things): basic tactics -- flank, pull down the wounded, coordinate as a pack, break off when losing. Still no buffs, no bargaining, no complex plans.\n- SAPIENT / TRAINED (people, intelligent monsters, disciplined soldiers): the full repertoire. They assess, they use abilities, and they NEVER default to just attacking -- they buff, heal, shield and protect allies, defend themselves, control terrain, feint, subdue, and retreat, surrender, or parley when it serves them. A trained fighter guards a flank and covers a caster; a mage opens with a buff or a control effect, not a wand-tap; a captain gives orders that shape the whole line.\n\nNEVER JUST ATTACK (for anything that can think)\nFor any sapient or trained combatant, a round of nothing but \"attacks X\" is a failure. Each round, ask what the smart move is: is an ally worth protecting, a wound worth finishing, a buff worth casting, ground worth taking, a caster worth interrupting, a fight worth leaving? Use the whole kit. Only the mindless are excused from this -- they are supposed to be simple.\n\nTHE ARC OF A FIGHT\n- Full HP: professional composure, disciplined tactics.\n- Below half: the character's true nature surfaces -- desperation, fury, cunning, or cowardice. Earned by attrition, never declared round one.\n- Near death: last stands, surrender, flight, or bargaining, per character.\n- Grouped combatants coordinate: pin and flank, a leader's orders shape the followers, buffs land before strikes.\n- Outmatched combatants disengage -- surrender, flee, bargain, or freeze. Trivials break earliest, the disciplined and the fanatical hold longest.\n- Terrain matters when someone uses it: cover, elevation, chokepoints, hazards. Elite minds shape the ground; they do not just fight on it.",
    "combat_output": "## OUTPUT FORMAT -- COMBAT CHANNEL\nCombat intents do NOT use the social pipe grammar. Mechanics live in structured fields the engine reads to resolve the round; \"action\" is tight prose (1-2 sentences) describing the move, with any vocalization folded in. Do not restate targets or damage in the action -- the fields are the source of truth.\n\nFIELDS\n- characterName: the acting combatant, exact allowed name.\n- type: one of attack, defend, buff, heal, subdue, preventAttack, mockAttack, evade.\n- action: prose, what the move looks like.\n- targets: array of exact allowed names.\n- damageType (attacks): one of the world damage types (piercing, slashing, fire, arcane, shadow, etc.).\n- impact (attacks): rough effort/strength of the move as a number.\n\nCHOOSING THE TYPE (do not default to attack)\n- attack: offensive strike or spell -- set damageType and impact.\n- defend: brace, guard, take cover, protect an ally.\n- evade: dodge, dive clear, break line of sight.\n- buff: any stat change (see below) -- resistances, vulnerabilities, skill up/down.\n- heal: restore an ally or self.\n- subdue: capture or incapacitate without killing.\n- preventAttack: interrupt, disrupt, or lock down an enemy's action.\n- mockAttack: feint, taunt, or bait.\n\nTHE BUFF FORMAT (one type, four shapes via buffType)\ntype=\"buff\" for EVERY stat change -- positive or negative, self or enemy. Fields: buffType, buffTarget, buffAmount, buffDuration (rounds), plus targets.\n- Resistance: buffType \"resistance\", buffTarget a damage type, buffAmount the tier. Grants damage resistance.\n  e.g. buffType:\"resistance\", buffTarget:\"fire\", buffAmount:1, buffDuration:3\n- Vulnerability: buffType \"vulnerability\", buffTarget a damage type. Makes the target take more of that damage (a debuff, still type=\"buff\").\n  e.g. buffType:\"vulnerability\", buffTarget:\"fire\", buffAmount:1, buffDuration:3\n- Skill up: buffType \"skill\", buffTarget a REAL combat skill name, positive buffAmount. Strengthens that skill.\n  e.g. buffType:\"skill\", buffTarget:\"great weapons\", buffAmount:2, buffDuration:3\n- Skill down: same as skill up but NEGATIVE buffAmount, aimed at an enemy. Weakens that skill.\n  e.g. buffType:\"skill\", buffTarget:\"martial weapons\", buffAmount:-2, buffDuration:3\nDebuffs are just buffs pointed at an enemy: vulnerability, or skill with a negative amount. buffTarget must be a real damage type (resistance/vulnerability) or a real skill (skill).\n\nEXAMPLES (abridged)\n- {\"type\":\"attack\",\"characterName\":\"Kaelen Vane\",\"action\":\"lunges in with the notched blade at her exposed side\",\"targets\":[\"Vennah\"],\"damageType\":\"slashing\",\"impact\":100}\n- {\"type\":\"buff\",\"characterName\":\"Mage\",\"action\":\"weaves a shimmering ward over the frontline fighter\",\"targets\":[\"Garrick\"],\"buffType\":\"resistance\",\"buffTarget\":\"fire\",\"buffAmount\":1,\"buffDuration\":3}\n- {\"type\":\"heal\",\"characterName\":\"Priest\",\"action\":\"presses light into the wounded scout's shoulder\",\"targets\":[\"Scout\"]}"
